    "https://ats.talentadore.com/apply/doctoral-researcher-in-prior-immunity-as-a-disease-modulator-eu-doctoral-network-detective/Dw1jwB?UTUID=21646": {
      "title": "Doctoral Researcher in Prior immunity as a disease modulator (EU Doctoral Network DETECTIVE)",
      "description": "Doctoral Researcher in Prior immunity as a disease modulator (EU Doctoral Network DETECTIVE) | Institute of Biomedicine JavaScript is disabled. Please enable JavaScript for the best recruiting experience. Apply for this position Doctoral Researcher in Prior immunity as a disease modulator (EU Doctoral Network DETECTIVE) About the DETECTIVE Doctoral Network DETECTIVE (Data-driven Excellence in Training of Epidemiological, Clinical, Technological and Innovative Virus Experts) is a Horizon Europe Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions (MSCA) Doctoral Network coordinated by the University of Turku. The network brings together leading academic, public health, clinical, and industrial partners across Europe to train the next generation of researchers in epidemic intelligence, emerging virus surveillance, and pandemic preparedness. DETECTIVE addresses the growing challenge of emerging and re-emerging viral threats by combining expertise in virology, genomics, epidemiology, diagnostics, data science, and public health. Through an integrated programme of research, training, and international mobility, doctoral candidates will develop expertise across the full pathway from pathogen detection to evidence-based public health decision-making. DETECTIVE is funded through the Horizon Europe Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Networks programme (Grant Agreement No. 101312731). The network will recruit 14 doctoral candidates across partner institutions in Europe. Three of these positions will be hosted at the University of Turku, while additional positions will be based at partner organisations in Finland, Denmark, the Netherlands, Spain, the United Kingdom, Austria, and Switzerland. Research areas within the network include: Viral diagnostics and surveillance Virus evolution and genomics Host-pathogen interactions Emerging and re-emerging viral infections Epidemiology and disease modelling Data-driven epidemic intelligence Public health preparedness and response Advanced laboratory and experimental models For more information, please visit: virusdetective.eu Job description This doctoral project focuses on investigating the mechanisms underlying the re-emergence of poliovirus (PV) in Europe and globally, with particular emphasis on the evolution of vaccine-derived polioviruses (VDPVs) and their propensity to recombine with other non-pathogenic enteroviruses belonging to species C ( Enterovirus coxsackiepol ). By integrating virological, immunological, molecular, and epidemiological approaches, the project aims to identify the viral and host factors that drive recombination events and facilitate the emergence and spread of vaccine-derived strains in under-immunised populations. The findings will contribute to a better understanding of poliovirus evolution, transmission dynamics, and outbreak preparedness, supporting global efforts towards polio eradication. The position will commence on 1 March 2027 and is funded for 36 months. The doctoral candidate will be supervised by Professor Peter Simmonds and Professor Heli Harvala at the University of Turku, with additional scientific mentoring from Dr Carita Savolainen-Kopra (Finnish Institute for Health and Welfare (THL), Finland) and Professor Nicolas Grassly (Imperial College London, UK). The position includes international secondments at THL Helsinki (Finland) and Imperial College London (UK), providing training in both public health and academic research environments. At the University of Turku, DETECTIVE is looking for a Doctoral Candidate (DC2) to contribute to Work Package 2. As DC2, your key responsibilities include: Constructing replication-competent molecular clones and poliovirus-derived replicons incorporating non-structural gene sequences from wild-type, vaccine-derived, and non-polio species C enteroviruses. Investigating replication kinetics and relative fitness of constructed replicons, Sabin vaccine strains, and vaccine-derived poliovirus variants. Producing recombinant poliovirus proteins for immunological studies. Conducting T-cell immunity assays using recombinant poliovirus proteins and peptides as antigens. Investigating the virological and immunological mechanisms that contribute to poliovirus recombination and the emergence of vaccine-derived strains. Collaborating with international research partners and contributing to scientific publications and presentations. This position combines virology, molecular biology, immunology, epidemiology, and international collaboration to advance the understanding of poliovirus evolution, vaccine-derived virus emergence, and the factors influencing infectious disease transmission and control. The doctoral researcher position requires working in Finland and the place of work is Turku. The application deadline is 8 October 2026 at 23:59 (EEST). Who we are looking for The position is intended for full-time doctoral dissertation research and postgraduate studies. Requirements Applicants must: Hold a Master's degree (or equivalent) in biomedical sciences, microbiology, virology, immunology, epidemiology, or a related field. Be eligible for doctoral studies at the University of Turku. You are required to have completed a relevant Master’s degree awarded by a university or a university of applied sciences, or a relevant study programme abroad which in the awarding country gives eligibility for the corresponding level of higher education (Universities Act 558/2009, 37§). Demonstrate a strong interest in infectious disease research and emerging pathogens. Possess excellent written and oral communication skills in English. Be able to work effectively in an interdisciplinary and international research environment. Comply with MSCA Doctoral Network eligibility requirements and mobility rules. Mobility rule: Applicants must not have resided or carried out their main activity (work, studies, etc.) in Finland for more than 12 months during the 36 months immediately preceding recruitment (1 March 2027). Doctoral Degree Requirement: Applicants must not hold a doctoral degree at the time of recruitment. Desirable qualifications Experience in one or more of the following areas is considered an advantage: Experience in molecular biology, virology, serology, genomics, or bioinformatics. Experience handling epidemiological or health-related datasets. Previous laboratory experience in infectious disease research. Doctoral Studies and Admission Requirements The selected candidate must be eligible for doctoral studies at the University of Turku and the Turku Doctoral Programme of Molecular Medicine (TuDMM) . Admission to doctoral studies at the University of Turku is a prerequisite for appointment to the position. Applicants who do not already hold the right to pursue a doctoral degree at the University of Turku must apply for doctoral study rights in accordance with the Faculty of Medicine and TuDMM admission requirements. The doctoral study right will be granted through a separate decision outside the regular doctoral admission rounds. Selected applicants must meet all eligibility requirements for doctoral studies, including degree eligibility, language proficiency requirements, and submission of all required supporting documents. Further information: Faculty of Medicine Doctoral Admissions Process How to Apply for Doctoral Study Rights at the Faculty of Medicine Language requirements for doctoral studies Turku Doctoral Programme of Molecular Medicine Admission We value collegiality and promote equality, diversity, and inclusivity. We encourage all qualified applicants with different backgrounds to apply for our open positions. What We Offer A fully funded 36-month doctoral researcher position at the University of Turku. A competitive salary and comprehensive employment benefits in accordance with MSCA Doctoral Network regulations and University of Turku employment conditions. Mobility and family allowances in accordance with MSCA regulations, where applicable. Participation in an international Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Network. International mobility, networking, and career development opportunities. Planned secondments in both academic and industrial settings. Participation in DETECTIVE-wide training schools covering: Foundations in Virology, Bioinformatics and Epidemiology Advanced Integration of Scientific, Diagnostic and Public Health Virology Translational Virology and Pandemic Preparedness Individual career development planning and mentoring. Access to state-of-the-art research infrastructure and expert support services. Access to university wellbeing and sports services. Benefits We offer you responsible and interesting tasks and the opportunity to develop your professional skills in a versatile operating environment. You get a professional and inspiring team to support your work. We take care of your well-being at work and the functionality of your everyday life, e.g. with the help of flexible working hours and our well-being services. Possibilities to develop your expertise – We encourage our personnel to develop their professional expertise by offering versatile training and competence development possibilities which employees can take advantage of during their working hours. Also, the course offering of Open University is free of charge for our personnel. Your well-being is important to us! – In addition to comprehensive occupational health care, our own well-being services monitor, support and develop the well-being of our personnel. You are entitled to use the CampusSport’s services at a personnel price. We support your work-life balance – Flexible working hours and extensive remote work options help you balance work and leisure time. An inspiring and supportive work community – We encourage each other and value working together! Please read more about University of Turku as an employer from our Come work with us! pages. The European Commission has awarded the University of Turku the right to use the HR Excellence in Research quality label . The label is a token of the University's commitment to continuous development of the position and working conditions of researchers according to the guidelines set forth in the European Charter for Researchers. The university offers good support and orientation for international hires. Please learn more about the Finnish culture and relocation to Finland: City of Turku Welcome visitor International House of Turku Working culture: infoFinland.fi This is Finland Careers in Southwest Finland Finland - a superpower of education and innovations | University of Turku (utu.fi) The University of Turku is among the top of the world’s universities in most rankings and among the best universities in the world in certain fields. Our high-quality basic research, a multidisciplinary research profile and close cooperation in research infrastructures creates the basis for international top research. Read more about University of Turku in international university rankings. The University of Turku has several high-quality research infrastructures required for cutting-edge research. Learn more about the roadmap of research infrastructures and how the research infrastructure and expert teams offer tailored solutions for businesses and organizations . Salary and trial period Doctoral candidates will be employed full-time for three years in accordance with the rules of the Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions (MSCA) Doctoral Networks programme. The financial package includes a living allowance of €4,010 per month (subject to the MSCA country correction coefficient applicable to Finland), a mobility allowance of €710 per month, and, where applicable, a family allowance of €660 per month. The actual gross and net salary will depend on the applicable country correction coefficient, local taxation, statutory social security contributions, and institutional employment policies. The exact salary will be confirmed upon appointment. The position is subject to a six-month probationary period. How to apply Please submit your application through the University of Turku electronic recruitment system by 8 October 2026 at 23:59 (Europe/Helsinki). Please write your application and accompanying documents in English. Please ensure that all attachments are high-quality, easy-to-read, full-colour scans or photographs of the complete document. All attachments must be submitted in PDF format. Attachments that do not meet these requirements may be rejected. Name each file so that the file name clearly indicates both your name and the document type (e.g. surname-firstname-CV). The application must include: 1. Curriculum Vitae (maximum 2 pages) The applicant’s CV should include education, relevant work and research experience, scientific achievements, and publications (if any). Please use Europass CV format, available also at: https://europass.europa.eu/en/create-europass-cv. 2. A Motivation Letter (maximum 1 page) Applicants should describe their motivation for applying, relevant qualifications and experience, research interests, and how their background aligns with the objectives of the doctoral project and the DETECTIVE Doctoral Network. 3. Degree certificate(s) and a transcript of study records Attach to the electronic application form: the scanned or electronic original certificate of the higher university degree (e.g. Master’s degree), the scanned or electronic original transcript of study records of the higher university degree, other study certificates relevant to the selection criteria (for example, studies completed outside the degree that relate to the doctoral degree you are applying for). If the documents are not in Finnish, Swedish or English, you must also submit an official translation into one of those languages; an official translation is accepted if issued by the degree awarding university or produced by an authorised translator. A Diploma Supplement (DS) issued by a European higher education institution may be accepted instead of an English translation of the degree certificate and transcript, provided it contains the details of the study records. 4. Details of one referee Provide the name, position, organisation, and contact details of one referee. 5. Any other relevant supporting documents that may influence the filling of the position (optional) Please note that applicants must also complete the DETECTIVE online application form available at virusdetective.eu or Webropol link . Candidates will be shortlisted and invited to participate in a two-stage interview process. The first round of online interviews will be conducted between 30 October and 9 November 2026. Candidates who progress to the next stage will be invited to a second round of interviews during November 2026. The recruitment process is expected to be completed by December 2026. Important Information Regarding Doctoral Study Rights The information in this section applies only to candidates selected for the position and is not required at the application stage. Please note that employment cannot commence on 1 March 2027 unless the selected candidate has been granted the right to pursue doctoral studies at the University of Turku. Admission to doctoral studies is a prerequisite for employment. You must meet the faculty and doctoral programme requirements for granting the right to pursue a doctoral degree. Carefully read the application guidelines of the Medical Faculty and the requirements of the Turku Doctoral Programme of Molecular Medicine . Applicants selected for the position will be required to prepare, together with their supervisors, the documents necessary for the doctoral study right application, including: Supervision and study plan – the Faculty’s form (Fill in electronically, print and sign together with the supervisor/-s and the head of subject, see: Subjects in doctoral training and heads of doctoral subject ). ​​​​​​ Please refer to the faculty website for more detailed instructions. Research plan (maximum three pages , excluding references; Arial, font size 12). The research plan is recommended to be written in English and should include the following sections: abstract, background and significance, aims and hypotheses, implementation plan, research environment, timetable, and funding plan. When preparing the research plan you must follow the principles of good scientific practice ( link to the Finnish National Board on Research Integrity (TENK’s) guidelines on good scientific practice ). The research plan may be checked with the Turnitin OriginalityCheck plagiarism‑detection system. Any use of artificial intelligence (AI) as an aid in preparing the research plan must be clearly declared in the application. Degree certificate and transcript of study records The MSc degree requirement (or equivalent qualification) must be fulfilled, and the degree certificate issued, before the study right can be granted. Proof of language proficiency, where applicable . Please refer to the Graduate School's webpage for information on language requirements and accepted forms of proof . Please note that the documents required for obtaining the right to pursue doctoral studies are not required at the application stage. Further information This project is funded by the European Union (DETECTIVE, 101312731). Views and opinions expressed are however those of the author(s) only and do not necessarily reflect those of the European Union or the Research Executive Agency. Neither the European Union nor the granting authority can be held responsible for them. Scientific questions regarding the DETECTIVE Doctoral Network and the doctoral project, please contact Professor Heli Harvala, heli.harvala@utu.fi or Research Coordinator Outi Lyytinen outi.lyytinen@utu.fi . Questions related to the general application process, please contact HR Specialist Kristiina Nuutila kristiina.nuutila@utu.fi or Research Coordinator Outi Lyytinen outi.lyytinen@utu.fi Questions regarding study rights and admissions, please contact Coordinator, Verna Louhivuori vtlouh@utu.fi Apply between and Institute of Biomedicine Research at the Institute of Biomedicine is divided into three functional units: Infection and Immunity, Integrative Physiology and Pharmacology, and Cancer research. Research at our Institute includes both biomedical basic research and translational research, and the unit division expresses our research priorities. The Faculty of Medicine is a high level, international and innovative research and education unit in biomedicine, clinical medicine, dentistry and health and nursing sciences, as well as a major social contributor to health promotion, disease genesis, in the research of diagnostics and treatment and in the development of the health service and education system. The faculty provides research-based multidisciplinary undergraduate education in educational programs that educate diverse and critically minded specialists. The research is multidisciplinary and results in new information aimed at promoting the health of the population and better patient care. The University of Turku is an inspiring and international academic community of 25,000 students and staff in Southwest Finland. We build a sustainable future with multidisciplinary research, education, and collaboration. With us, your work will have a significant impact and relevance in the changing world. See our open vacancies Homepage LinkedIn Facebook Youtube Instagram Email: heli.harvala@utu.fi Information for the applicant The application form cannot be saved as a draft. However, after submitting you can return to edit your application via the link you receive by email. The application can be edited until the application deadline. You have 6 hours to complete your application before the timeout. Instructions for submitting a job application Apply for this position Doctoral Researcher in Prior immunity as a disease modulator (EU Doctoral Network DETECTIVE) Add your picture Are you sure you want to withdraw this appl"
