      "title": "Doctoral Researcher in Identification of genetic and host markers for severe infection (EU Doctoral Network DETECTIVE)",
      "description": "Doctoral Researcher in Identification of genetic and host markers for severe infection (EU Doctoral Network DETECTIVE) | Institute of Biomedicine JavaScript is disabled. Please enable JavaScript for the best recruiting experience. Apply for this position Doctoral Researcher in Identification of genetic and host markers for severe infection (EU Doctoral Network DETECTIVE) About the DETECTIVE Doctoral Network DETECTIVE (Data-driven Excellence in Training of Epidemiological, Clinical, Technological and Innovative Virus Experts) is a Horizon Europe Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions (MSCA) Doctoral Network coordinated by the University of Turku. The network brings together leading academic, public health, clinical, and industrial partners across Europe to train the next generation of researchers in epidemic intelligence, emerging virus surveillance, and pandemic preparedness. DETECTIVE addresses the growing challenge of emerging and re-emerging viral threats by combining expertise in virology, genomics, epidemiology, diagnostics, data science, and public health. Through an integrated programme of research, training, and international mobility, doctoral candidates will develop expertise across the full pathway from pathogen detection to evidence-based public health decision-making. DETECTIVE is funded through the Horizon Europe Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Networks programme (Grant Agreement No. 101312731). The network will recruit 14 doctoral candidates across partner institutions in Europe. Three of these positions will be hosted at the University of Turku, while additional positions will be based at partner organisations in Finland, Denmark, the Netherlands, Spain, the United Kingdom, Austria, and Switzerland. Research areas within the network include: Viral diagnostics and surveillance Virus evolution and genomics Host-pathogen interactions Emerging and re-emerging viral infections Epidemiology and disease modelling Data-driven epidemic intelligence Public health preparedness and response Advanced laboratory and experimental models For more information, please visit: virusdetective.eu Job description This doctoral project focuses on identifying host factors associated with susceptibility to, and severity of, viral respiratory and neurological infections. By integrating transcriptomic, proteomic, metabolomic, and genetic data from large-scale clinical cohorts, the project aims to improve understanding of host responses to infection and uncover biomarkers and genetic risk factors associated with severe disease outcomes. The findings will contribute to the development of improved approaches for risk assessment, disease prediction, and epidemic preparedness. The position will commence on 1 March 2027 and is funded for 36 months. The doctoral candidate will be supervised by Professor Heli Harvala and Professor Peter Simmonds at the University of Turku, with additional scientific mentoring from Eero Vesterinen (Bioname, Finland) and Dr Jelte Helfferich (University Medical Centre Groningen, the Netherlands). The position includes international secondments at Bioname (Finland) and the University Medical Centre Groningen (the Netherlands), providing training in both academic and industrial research environments. At the University of Turku, DETECTIVE is looking for a Doctoral Candidate (DC1) to contribute to Work Package 2. As DC1, your key responsibilities include: Investigating changes in transcriptomic, proteomic, and metabolomic profiles before, during, and after respiratory infections to identify biomarkers associated with viral infection. Quantifying host response patterns and determining markers associated with severe respiratory and neurological infections using large cohorts of archived clinical samples. Identifying genetic risk factors associated with severe neurological and/or respiratory infections through integration of clinical data with host genotype information from the FinnGen database. Comparing genetic susceptibility markers with transcriptomic, proteomic, and metabolomic profiles to improve understanding of the biological mechanisms underlying severe infection. Integrating multi-omics, genetic, and clinical datasets to identify predictors of disease severity and host susceptibility. Collaborating with international research partners and contributing to scientific publications and presentations. This position combines molecular epidemiology, genomics, bioinformatics, data analysis, and international collaboration to advance the understanding of host determinants of severe infectious diseases. The doctoral researcher position requires working in Finland and the place of work is Turku. The application deadline is 8 October 2026 at 23:59 (EEST). Who we are looking for The position is intended for full-time doctoral dissertation research and postgraduate studies. Requirements Applicants must: Hold a Master's degree (or equivalent) in biomedical sciences, microbiology, virology, immunology, epidemiology, or a related field. Be eligible for doctoral studies at the University of Turku. You are required to have completed a relevant Master’s degree awarded by a university or a university of applied sciences, or a relevant study programme abroad which in the awarding country gives eligibility for the corresponding level of higher education (Universities Act 558/2009, 37§). Demonstrate a strong interest in infectious disease research and emerging pathogens. Possess excellent written and oral communication skills in English. Be able to work effectively in an interdisciplinary and international research environment. Comply with MSCA Doctoral Network eligibility requirements and mobility rules. Mobility rule: Applicants must not have resided or carried out their main activity (work, studies, etc.) in Finland for more than 12 months during the 36 months immediately preceding recruitment (1 March 2027). Doctoral Degree Requirement: Applicants must not hold a doctoral